                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Tiered Management Solves More Problems Than You Think</h2>
<p>Many teams complain about managing too many accounts. The real issue isn't quantity—it's <strong>the lack of classification standards</strong>. A $500/day utility app account and a $5,000/day gaming account managed under the same logic will inevitably create chaos: test budgets get crowded out, risk responses lag, and financial reconciliation relies on manual spreadsheet extraction.</p>
<p>The core value of tiered account management boils down to three points: <strong>more efficient resource allocation, faster risk response, and clearer financial tracking.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Tier 1: Stratify by Campaign Stage</h2>
<p>This is the most fundamental approach and the easiest for most teams to implement.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Characteristics</th>
<th>Management Focus</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Test Accounts</strong></td>
<td>New accounts or testing phase, $100-500/day spend</td>
<td>Monitor CTR and CPI fluctuations, isolate budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Scale Accounts</strong></td>
<td>Validated creatives, stable spend over $1,000/day</td>
<td>Focus on ROAS and account balance alerts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Maintenance Accounts</strong></td>
<td>Stable but low ROI, or paused/dormant</td>
<td>Regular reviews to avoid resource waste</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>One cross-border e-commerce team previously managed all accounts together. Their test account creatives had just started showing data but were crowded out by scale account budgets. After separation, test accounts received isolated budget caps, improving conversion rates by 23%.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Tier 2: Stratify by Risk Level</h2>
<p>Account bans, ad disapprovals, and balance anomalies are common in overseas advertising. Tiered management helps the risk team locate issues faster.</p>
<p><strong>High-Risk Accounts</strong>: Frequently trigger platform review, ad disapproval rate exceeds 15%, or daily spend fluctuates over 50%. These require <strong>daily inspections</strong> with automated alert thresholds.</p>
<p><strong>Medium-Risk Accounts</strong>: Running new creative materials or recently modified targeting/regions. These need <strong>weekly reviews</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Low-Risk Accounts</strong>: Long-term stable performance with infrequent creative changes. These can be <strong>monthly check-ins</strong>, freeing up attention for high-risk accounts.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Tier 3: Stratify by Financial Ownership</h2>
<p>This layer is overlooked by many teams but directly impacts financial reconciliation efficiency.</p>
<p><strong>By Product Line</strong>: Different product lines get separate account groups, matching their respective budgets and cost centers.</p>
<p><strong>By Platform</strong>: Google, Meta, TikTok, Pinterest—each platform gets independent accounts and separate funding to avoid cross-platform fund mixing.</p>
<p><strong>By Partnership Model</strong>: Direct vs. agency accounts, parent vs. sub-accounts have different financial ownership and reconciliation logic.</p>
<p>After tiering, month-end reconciliation becomes straightforward—just export by tier instead of manually extracting data from dozens of accounts. One gaming overseas team reduced their reconciliation time from 2 days to 4 hours using this approach.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three Key Actions to Implement Tiered Management</h2>
<h3>1. Build Account Profiles</h3>
<p>Every account needs an "ID card" documenting: account opening date, platform, product line, current status (testing/scaling/maintenance), responsible person, and recharge history.</p>
<h3>2. Set Up Automated Alerts</h3>
<p>Don't rely on manual monitoring. Configure automated alerts for high-risk accounts: low balance thresholds, abnormal spend velocity, ad disapprovals. Major advertising platforms and agency backends support basic automated notifications.</p>
<h3>3. Conduct Regular Reviews and Tier Adjustments</h3>
<p>Tiering isn't a one-time setup. Promote test accounts to scaling tier when they show stable performance; demote underperforming scale accounts to maintenance. Review account statuses bi-weekly to maintain dynamic updates.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Account tiered management isn't some advanced management theory—it's the <strong>path of least resistance from chaos to order</strong>. Stratify accounts by stage, risk, and financial ownership, then reinforce with profiles, alerts, and reviews. The implementation barrier is low, but it significantly improves overall campaign efficiency.</p>
<p>If you're still managing all accounts with a single spreadsheet, it's time for a change.</p>
